  "classify": "Motion-designed product film with a meta frame (the video plays inside the product's own player). Conventions: the artifact as hero, comments as direction, connector logo row, a two-beat end card.",
  "diagnose": [
    {
      "observation": "Comments in the margin say what the next shot should do.",
      "principle": "Show the cause next to the effect: the instruction and its result are on screen together, so the edit reads as obeying it."
    },
    {
      "observation": "The end card is typed and selected like the product's own text.",
      "principle": "The medium is the message: even the sign-off is made in the tool."
    },
    {
      "observation": "Most cuts are dissolves (24 of 47).",
      "principle": "Crossfades keep one continuous world across a long film; hard cuts are saved for the export beat."
    }
  ],
  "teaches": [
    {
      "tag": "new",
      "text": "Direction notes ('hold a beat longer') are how a motion edit is briefed; cutscene's scene hints (`act.focus`, `act.wide`) are the same idea in code.",
      "setting": "act.focus / act.wide"
    },
    {
      "tag": "confirms",
      "text": "End on a claim card, then the logo.",
      "setting": "CARD"
    },
    {
      "tag": "confirms",
      "text": "Pushes of ×1.5–1.7 for a working shot.",
      "setting": "CAMERA.working"
    }
  ],
  "critique": {
    "like": "The artifact is the hero; the presenter is shown small, inside a player, so the product never gives up the frame.",
    "wish": "At 81 s it explains rather than shows: the voice-over carries ideas the picture already proved.",
    "wonder": "Could a cutscene reel take its hints as plain-language notes on marks ('hold here a beat') and turn them into holds and pushes?"
  },
